TERMIC AMIGA WORKSPACE

Native MUI development workspace for AmigaOS 3.x: projects and independent
source-copy tasks, embedded source editing, offline Tiny and cloud agents,
local builds, program execution, AmigaDOS commands and local Git review.
The application and compiler run on the Amiga. No Mac, Docker, DevBench or
LAN model server is needed at runtime.

OFFLINE TINY
Tiny 2.1 is the default for tasks without a saved agent selection. Existing
selections and credentials are preserved. Ask one short question, up to
72 printable ASCII characters, such as "What is the capital of Peru?".
Tiny runs on the Amiga 68k CPU using Tools/Tiny/tiny68k and Tools/Tiny/tiny.t68.
Both are bundled: no internet connection, API key, Mac proxy or ZZ9000 is
needed for inference. The reply appears in Agent activity. There is no
automatic cloud fallback. Tiny is a small factual question-answering model;
it cannot edit files or run builds. Termic adds offline guide search,
read-only project access, saved conversation context and explicit handoff.
Type /help with Tiny selected. Examples: /docs yolo, /files, /read main.c,
/history, /recall MUI, /remember Use MUI 5, /forget and /agents.
Use /handoff ID request with an ID from /agents to send the request and
recent saved context to Codex, Claude or Cursor through the host AI Proxy.
Target profile permissions apply. Tiny stays selected and task text is kept.
Memory stores 8 shortened turns and 4096 characters of notes per task.
It supports recall/handoff, not neural conversation reasoning. /forget
keeps the activity transcript. See Help > Contents > Offline Tiny for
all commands, limits and exactly what handoff sends.
Stop requests cooperative cancellation.
TINY 2.1 is by WollyMonjac; the Amiga engine is SkiltonUSA/tiny68k.
Keep Tools/Tiny/LICENSE and DATA_LICENSES.md with the model. See the Tiny
node in Termic.guide for usage and the upstream training sources.

REQUIREMENTS
68020+, AmigaOS 3.2, MUI 5.0, TextEditor.mcc 15.56, Fast RAM, VBCC and NDK.
Cloud access needs Roadshow or compatible bsdsocket.library, AmiSSL with CA
certificates, an accurate clock and an OpenAI API key. The bundled native
Git client was verified with AmiSSL 5.27; install that runtime from its
upstream distribution. The older 5.23 developer headers remain compatible.
Git repositories require long filenames (PFS3/SFS or FFS with long names).

INSTALL
Unpack into a new drawer. In an Amiga Shell:
  Assign Termic: Work:TermicNative
  Execute Termic:InstallSDK
  Execute Termic:InstallUI
  Execute Termic:BuildNative
  Termic:TermicNative --icon Termic:TermicNative
Replace Work:TermicNative with your chosen drawer, then double-click the
TermicNative icon. The application assigns Termic: to its own drawer.
For a Shell launch, set Stack 262144 before running Termic:TermicNative.
The Workbench icon already requests this stack. MUI 5 is supported; its
Title class provides the workspace tabs with the restored application font.
InstallUI adds TextEditor only if absent; it never replaces your MUI core.
SDK archives include their original licences. Startup files are not edited.
The AmiSSL 5.27 runtime archive is in SDK/amissl527.lha; extract it into a
temporary drawer and run its original Install-AmiSSL installer if needed.

On an FFS volume with 30-character filenames, optionally create independent
64 MiB project storage after InstallUI and BuildNative:
  Termic:TermicStorage --create
This creates a new Projects.hdf file and initializes only that new image.
It refuses an existing image or a disk-image unit owned by another device.
Settings then uses TMGIT:Projects; Termic mounts that image automatically.
No existing partition is formatted and no startup file is changed. Unit 7
of diskimage.device is reserved for this optional volume. Use another
long-filename drawer in Settings if the unit is needed by another app.
To back up the image, close active jobs and use a Shell outside TMGIT:
  Termic:TermicStorage --eject
Copy Projects.hdf, then run Termic:TermicStorage to mount it again.

VERSION AND ABOUT
The native Amiga version is defined in Source/version.h, independently of
the desktop Termic version. The workspace header shows the native version.
Choose Project > About... or press Shift-Help for the version, build date,
credits, licence and source/support links. Escape, Return or Close dismiss
that window. About can be reopened without restarting the workspace.
All three executables support --version and AmigaDOS Version FILE FULL:
  Termic:TermicNative --version
  Version Termic:TermicNative FILE FULL
The traditional Amiga tag uses major.minor; FULL also shows the complete
native version and its Beta designation.

WORKSPACE
Native MUI tabs select Agent, Editor, Build / CLI, Changes, Settings and Processors.
At 340x280 a top selector also offers Projects, Tasks, Files and CLI as
full-width panes. At 800x500 and above, Projects/Tasks sit on the left,
the tabbed work page in the centre, and Files above CLI on the right.
Task instructions sit above a single row of agent controls below the
activity log. The header shows the version and Build, Run, Settings, About and Quit icons.
Projects uses one compact icon row: New, Rename and Import Folder.
Tasks uses one compact icon row: New, Rename, Kanban, Queue and Mark Done.
Hover over an icon for its full action name. Click inside the source editor
to type code, then use its floppy Save icon beside Find/Next, F8 or
Right-Amiga-S. An asterisk before the filename marks unsaved changes.
Native balance dividers adjust column widths and the Files/CLI split.
F1-F5 select the work pages. Forms and tables scroll when necessary.
Helvetica 13 controls and Courier 13 source text match the reference UI,
with MUI font fallbacks if unavailable. Resizing preserves unsaved text
and cursor position. The source editor keeps its black syntax colours.

New project uses the clean Template drawer. Import source drawer copies
regular editable files into a managed project. New task copies the selected
workspace's source and build command into its own Tasks/task-NNNN drawer.
It does not copy Git internals, credentials or conversation IDs. These are
source snapshots, not Git worktrees. The installed amiga-git has no worktree
command. Original Project/main.c remains available as the starter project.
Names and task status are saved, and the last workspace reopens on launch.

EDITOR
Select a file to edit it in TextEditor.mcc. Use Project > Save and the Edit
menu for Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy and Paste. Find and Next remain below the editor. A successful save keeps
previous contents in file.bak. File/workspace switches save pending edits;
a failed save prevents switching. Source files are limited to 32 KiB.
The source editor has a black background and light plain text.
C/header files open with bright colours for keywords, functions, strings, numbers,
comments and preprocessor directives. Colours are applied when a file loads.
Ordinary Save preserves undo history. Saved source contains no style codes.
Literal ESC bytes in source are rejected to avoid interpreting them as styles.
Task instructions are edited in the Agent tab, not in a second editor copy.

BUILD AND RUN
Build uses the current workspace, produces Program, and displays diagnostics
in Build / CLI. The default compiles main.c with VBCC. Settings can save
a different build command per workspace, including additional C files and
libraries. It must produce Program.new; BuildProject promotes it on success.
Successful nested build scripts must reach EOF, without Quit 0. A premature
exit is rejected so that an old Program cannot be mistaken for a new build.
Upgrade TermicAgent and BuildProject together from the same release.
A failed build retains the last executable, so Run would launch that older build.
MUI headers and mui_compat.h are on the installed include search path.
Run launches the compiled program. Only an explicit Run or shell command
runs generated executables. The shell field executes AmigaDOS commands in
the selected workspace and displays their output here; it is not a PTY.

Different tasks can run separate workers, up to 16 per application. One
worker at a time owns each task; its editor is read-only during that job.
The UI sleeps when idle and refreshes output only while jobs are active.
Cancel signals the worker. Network waits observe cancellation; arbitrary
child programs may require their own Quit action before the worker returns.

GIT
Changes offers Init repository, Status, Diff, and Stage source and commit.
Set repository identity in the CLI panel before the first commit:
  Termic:Tools/amiga-git/git config user.name "Your Name"
  Termic:Tools/amiga-git/git config user.email you@example.com
The commit action stages existing C/H, Python, ARexx and text/Markdown files. Use the shell for deleted
files, branches, remotes, fetch/push, merge, stash and other Git operations.
Commit messages accept letters, digits, spaces and . , : / ( ) _ - .
New repositories ignore build products and Termic job metadata. Existing
.gitignore files are retained. No remote operation runs automatically.

AGENT
Enter task instructions, select a model and save an API key in Settings.
The secret field never loads the existing key. Leaving it blank preserves
that key. Start agent sends the task and requested source/compiler output
to OpenAI. The model may list/read/write C/H, Python, ARexx and text/Markdown files, build and execute. It has
no shell-command tool, and file tools reject device paths and traversal.
Completed conversation IDs persist per task; New conversation resets the
ID while retaining the transcript. API responses are stored for continuity.
Each run defaults to eight requests; profiles allow a limit of 1-32. API access and billing are required.
The default model is gpt-5-mini; choose a model available to your account.

The local Config/api-key file is plaintext on classic AmigaOS. It is sent
only as the HTTPS Authorization header to api.openai.com, never in command
arguments, prompts, logs or installation packages. TLS verifies certificates
and hostnames and requires TLS 1.2+. Test verified HTTPS expects HTTP 401
without a key; it does not establish authenticated model access.

SHORTCUTS
F1 Agent; F2 Editor; F3 Build / CLI; F4 Changes; F5 Settings.
F6 Build; F7 Run; F8 Save; F9 New task; F10 Quit.
Shift+F1 starts the agent; Shift+F2 focuses the new filename (Enter creates).
Shift+F3 focuses the shell command; Shift+F4 focuses the commit message.
Enter executes the shell command or commits from those fields.
Shift+F5 initializes Git; Shift+F6 shows status; Shift+F7 shows the diff;
Shift+F8 marks done; Shift+F9 creates a project; Shift+F10 cancels a job.
Text editing and clipboard keys follow TextEditor.mcc preferences.
Right-Amiga-1/2/3/4 select Agent/Editor/Build/Changes; Right-Amiga-comma
opens Settings. A single-line MUI field consumes some application keys;
Tab to a button first or choose a command using the mouse menu.

BOUNDARIES
This native implementation does not run the React/Tauri frontend or desktop
agent CLIs. Host profiles provide buffered live response text through the proxy.
There is no xterm terminal or race mode. Broadcast task copies, the parallel
queue and host worktree/PR controls are described below. Native tasks remain source copies.
The shell exposes the installed native Git client's additional commands.
Task instructions: 8 KiB. Individual source: 32 KiB. Agent compiler output:
16 KiB. HTTP response: 256 KiB. File list: 128 files, six directory levels.
Network deadline: 120 seconds after DNS, whose timeout is stack-controlled.
Long imports enumerate/copy synchronously; large trees can delay the UI.

Classic AmigaOS provides no process sandbox: compiler jobs and generated
programs retain normal OS access. Review changes before Run. After a worker
crash, inspect its job.log and remove its .busy drawer only when the worker
has stopped. One Termic installation should own the Termic: assign at a time.

ZZ9000
The existing Workbench RTG display is used. Compilation, TLS and file work
run on the 68k. No ARM payload, firmware or display-mode change is made.
ARM acceleration needs a measured workload, exclusive core ownership and
the verified shared-memory/cache contract from the hardware workspace.

VERIFICATION
Host: python3 scripts/amiga/test_native.py (synthetic API, ASan/UBSan).
Hardware: scripts/amiga/accept_mui.py through the existing DevBench MCP.
The hardware suite uses isolated fixtures and never submits a model request
or reads credentials. See docs/verification.md in the source repository for verified scope.

LICENSES
Termic: GNU AGPLv3 (LICENSE). cJSON: MIT (Source/vendor/LICENSE.cJSON).
AmiSSL bindings: Apache 2.0 (Source/vendor/LICENSE.AmiSSL).
MUI and TextEditor headers are obtained from their original SDK archives;
muimaster ABI bindings are in Source/mui_compat.h. TextEditor is LGPL:
https://github.com/amiga-mui/texteditor/tree/15.56
amiga-git is freeware; its unmodified archive includes licences and libgit2
patches. VBCC/NDK/MUI SDKs retain their own redistribution conditions.

HELP AND AMIGA CONVENTIONS
Press Help or choose Help > Contents... for the linked Termic.guide manual.
Keep it beside TermicNative. Help uses a native MUI 5 window with chapter
selection, Contents, Index, Back, previous/next chapter and text search.
The related-topic selector follows local AmigaGuide links. Escape closes help.
MultiView is optional for viewing the same file outside Termic.
Project/Edit menus provide Right-Amiga-N/O/S/Q and X/C/V/Z. Edit commands
use the focused text editor, with unavailable actions ghosted. MUI handles
single-line field shortcuts; TextEditor uses the shared IFF text clipboard.
Settings > Preferences has Save, Use and Cancel. Save writes Config and
ENV:Termic; Use writes ENV:Termic until reboot; Cancel discards unapplied
edits. Restart for a changed project drawer. Build commands save separately.
Keys saved with Use are plaintext in ENV:Termic/api-key.
MagicMenu is optional; no system menu patch is installed by Termic.

Main window controls
--------------------
Termic uses native MUI/Intuition window controls for dragging, sizing,
depth, zoom and closing. The layout adapts to the current window size.
No separate custom title bar or system font change is applied. Use
Settings > MUI Settings to adjust gadget appearance. Workspace fonts use
Helvetica/Courier when installed, otherwise the corresponding MUI roles.

AGENTS / CLI / SCRIPTS
Settings opens a profile manager for OpenAI API, CLI, ARexx and MicroPython
profiles. Add, duplicate, remove custom profiles or reset the built-ins.
Save persists settings; Use lasts until reboot; Cancel discards draft edits.
The Agent page selects the applied profile and optional native YOLO arguments.
Profiles have independent API keys and conversation history. Blank keys retain
the existing key; copied profiles need their own key. Read-only API access
disables edits and builds. Steps, output tokens and actual token reporting
are configurable. Environment applies to native command workers only.
Desktop CLI login accounts, Keychain roles and Docker are not implemented.

AMIGA MCP / CLI
Settings > Agent access / MCP enables the TermicMCP tools, disabled by default.
TermicMCP --stdio uses newline-delimited JSON-RPC; --request file --reply file
is the same dispatcher for scripts. The scripts/amiga/mcp_bridge.py connector
uses an existing DevBench MCP bridge for clients on another computer.
No new network listener, discovery or desktop pairing service is installed.
Tools list projects, create isolated source-copy tasks, send prompts, inspect
results, build, edit C/H files and keep scratchpads. Writes refuse busy tasks
and the editor-selected workspace, and only target MCP-created tasks.
See the linked Agent profiles and MCP chapters in Termic.guide.

CLI, AREXX AND MICROPYTHON
The panel below Files has a mode selector: CLI, ARexx or MicroPython. In CLI mode,
enter an AmigaDOS command. In script modes, enter the script path and arguments,
then choose Execute. Output is captured; interactive input uses a separate CLI.
For examples, choose ARexx with Termic:Examples/hello.rexx or MicroPython with
Termic:Examples/hello.py. Add ARexx / Add MicroPython creates a launch profile
with the corresponding example already selected. Change Default arguments to
run your own script. Add CLI opens a separate C:NewShell console.
The editor supports .py, .rexx and .rx alongside C/H and text files. C/H, Python and ARexx receive live lexical colours. Agent and MCP source
file tools support C/H, Python, ARexx and text/Markdown. Named project
guidance is readable but remains protected from agent writes.
No Termic-specific ARexx host commands added.
ARexx requires SYS:Rexxc/RX and RexxMast. MicroPython is installed under
Termic:Tools/MicroPython with its license, upstream README and provenance.
MicroPython runs on the 68k CPU; it is not full desktop CPython and does not
use ZZ9000 ARM offload. The host MCP connector still runs in host CPython.


PROMPTS AND PROJECT INSTRUCTIONS

Prompts beside the composer, Settings > Prompts, or Right Amiga P opens the
17 desktop Termic defaults. Edit, New, Duplicate, Remove, Reset, Restore,
Up/Down and Enabled manage the library. Save persists, Use lasts until reboot,
and Cancel discards edits. Insert + Use appends to the composer without
sending. Choose an agent and press Tab. Existing task text is preserved;
the combined composer limit is 8192 bytes. The native API has no Git/push,
browser or subagent tools; templates explain these execution limits.

Workspace > Install agent instructions adds missing AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md,
.agents/skills/termic/SKILL.md, .termic-version and two reference documents.
Existing files are preserved. New projects/tasks receive local copies.
The API worker includes AGENTS.md and SKILL.md, up to 8192 bytes each, on
its next request. Named guidance is readable but not writable through agent
file tools. The Files list lets you edit it yourself with backup saves.

Right Amiga K or Help > Keyboard shortcuts opens the black reference window.
Right Amiga I or Tools > Process Monitor selects the embedded Processors tab.
No separate monitor application or window is launched. Sampling pauses while
the tab is hidden; a previously confirmed service restart still completes.
Its optional ZZ9000 button shows/hides board information; it is off initially.
This display option does not enable ARM compute offload.
Default prompt keys: Tab sends, Shift Tab cycles profiles, Ctrl J adds a newline,
Ctrl G opens the editor, Ctrl R searches history, F4 opens Process Monitor,
and two Esc presses recall the last sent message. In an empty prompt,
/ opens commands, @ mentions the open file, ! focuses the CLI, and ? shows
shortcuts. Ctrl T focuses the transcript. There, F3 finds, PgUp/PgDn scroll,
Ctrl Home/End or Shift Alt comma/period go to top/latest, and Ctrl Space
starts line selection extended with Up/Down. Ctrl Space finishes selection;
Right Amiga C copies. Esc returns to the prompt. Ctrl C quits from prompt
or transcript with normal save handling. Code editor Tab is unchanged.
Configure... in the reference remaps prompt/transcript actions, with conflict
checks, Defaults and Save/Use/Cancel. Save writes Config/keymap.json with a backup;
Use lasts this session. Menu/editor keys and empty-prompt symbols stay fixed.
Image paste, reasoning keys and subagent switching are unavailable.

Common action buttons use icons with hover tooltips: floppy Save, triangle
Run, square Stop, hammer Build, gear Settings, magnifier Find, speech bubble
Prompts, and keyboard Shortcuts. Menu names and keyboard commands remain.
Dialog Save/Use/Cancel use floppy, check and cross icons.

CODEX ON MAC (CHATGPT SIGN-IN)
Settings > Agents / CLI / Scripts > Add Codex creates a Mac relay profile.
Start Termic Amiga Codex.command on the Mac, select Codex on Mac in the Amiga
launcher, and send with Tab. Credentials stay on the Mac. The relay uses the
existing DevBench MCP bridge and Codex app-server; no new network port opens.
One task at a time. Native C/H/ARexx read/write/build/run tools, file-access limits,
backup saves, transcript updates, Stop and conversation resumption apply.
Blank model uses Codex's default. API steps / relay calls limits tool calls;
API token limits, usage reporting and CLI arguments do not apply.
Install on the Mac: python3 scripts/amiga/install_codex_relay.py
Full setup and limits: docs/codex-relay.md in the source repository.

CLAUDE, CURSOR AND DESKTOP AI PROXY

Settings > Agents / CLI / Scripts now includes Add Claude and Add Cursor.
Enter a supported Model and save, then select the profile in the composer.
Open Termic AI Proxy on the Mac or Windows 11 host. The app provides Start,
Stop, sign-in and connection status without an open terminal. DevBench and
the provider CLI must be installed separately. Credentials remain on the host.
The on Mac profile names also work with a Windows proxy. One task at a time.
See docs/ai-proxy.md in the repository for downloads and setup.

LIVE AGENT FEEDBACK
Codex, Claude and Cursor host profiles show text while the response is being
generated. Small chunks are combined on the host before transfer; update
speed depends on the Amiga bridge. Tool start and return markers appear in
the transcript, and the CLI log shows native tool activity immediately.
Updated host proxies and workers negotiate alternating reply files to
avoid shell cleanup for each message. Older peers remain compatible.
Task pickup polls every half-second; live text batches every 0.2 seconds.
These improve bridge responsiveness, not the model's thinking speed.

Replies wrap to the transcript width. Activity opens at the latest line
and follows new output automatically. Selected text is held for copying;
releasing the selection catches up to the latest line.
The Agent page combines cyan [Agent] responses and amber [Log] worker
messages in one black activity pane. Live entries appear in polling order;
opening a saved task shows its transcript followed by its latest job log.
Selected text stays stable while updates arrive. The task
instruction box sits above one row containing the profile selector, YOLO
control and four action buttons.
The log comes from local job.log. Mac proxy diagnostics remain in the
AI Proxy app when the bridge cannot deliver them to the Amiga.
The direct API profile does not stream tokens.

AGENT EXECUTION FRAMEWORK
All agent providers share system_info, run_arexx, run_python and run_program.
Use system_info for detected CPU, memory, OS/library versions, RexxMast and
raw AutoConfig IDs. Machine model, CPU MHz and ARM availability are unknown.
Save a task .rexx/.rx file, then use run_arexx. For C, build successfully
in the same turn before run_program. Edits invalidate the successful build.
Results include success, actual exit_code, output, output_truncated and
cancelled. Returned output is limited to 16 KiB; .command.log holds the capture.
Read-only profiles reject writes, builds and execution. Runs wait for exit,
without interactive input or arguments. Stop is cooperative; programs that
ignore breaks can keep running. The host tool wait is 180 seconds. Do not
replay a timed-out execution automatically. Use CLI/Run for long-running apps.
After updating worker and proxy, choose New conversation for the new tools.
Long profile IDs use short session filenames to fit classic filesystems.
Short-ID conversations are retained; long-ID profiles start a fresh session
after this filename update.
Cursor collects results with await_operation in polls of up to 20 seconds.
The original operation continues between polls; polls do not repeat execution
or consume native tool calls. A running operation is not a successful result.
Stop during native build/file I/O now preserves normal task-lock and port cleanup.
Full details: docs/agent-execution.md in the source repository.

TASK BOARD, QUEUE, REVIEW AND NOTES
Right Amiga Y: Kanban board (Backlog, Needs attention, Working, Review,
Done, Archived). Drag idle tasks between columns or use the actions. Working
is assigned by actual jobs; queued/running tasks reject state changes.
Right Amiga J: persistent queue, one to four independent workers. Host proxy
requests stay serial. Failure pauses new launches; active work can finish.
Restart always pauses. Pause does not stop an active job.
Queue > Broadcast creates separate task copies for selected agent profiles,
with a shared instruction and YOLO off. Copies queue paused for inspection.
Right Amiga U: coloured unified diff on black, or Before / after; whole-file
accept/revert with backup. Large changes use bounded replacement alignment.
Later user edits are protected. ARexx/program side effects need Git review.
Right Amiga L: scratchpad notes shared with MCP, 32 KiB each, backup saves.
Right Amiga G: native branches and host worktree/GitHub PR controls.
Host operations need updated Termic AI Proxy, Git and authenticated gh.
Sync refuses dirty worktrees and does not delete host files absent on Amiga.
PR creation requires confirmation and uses host credentials.
Build / CLI compiler locations open the matching source line on double-click.
Full usage and limits: docs/task-workflow.md and the linked AmigaGuide topics.
TermicCheck reports runtime, SDK, Git, ARexx and MicroPython dependencies.
